# Image Depth Estimation

Monocular depth estimation project developed as a Bachelor thesis, focused on NYU Depth V2 and KITTI experiments. The main model uses a pretrained DenseNet-169 encoder with a lightweight decoder and skip connections to predict dense depth from a single RGB image.

## Highlights

- DenseNet-169 transfer-learning backbone for monocular depth prediction
- KITTI Eigen-style train and test split support
- NYU Depth V2 zip-based loader retained for earlier experiments
- Training loss combining BerHu depth loss, SSIM, and depth-gradient consistency
- Evaluation metrics: REL, RMSE, log10, and delta accuracy thresholds
- TensorBoard logging for losses, validation metrics, predictions, and error maps

## Project Structure

~~~text
configs/                         Training defaults
scripts/                         Dataset utility scripts
src/depth_estimation/
  data/                          NYU and KITTI data loading
  data/kitti/dataloader/         KITTI file loading and interpolation
  data/kitti/transforms/         KITTI image/depth transforms
  evaluation/                    Metrics and qualitative export scripts
  models/                        DenseNet-169 encoder-decoder model
  training/                      Training loop and loss functions
  visualization/                 TensorBoard and curve plotting helpers
~~~

Exploratory notebooks, TensorBoard event files, caches, local datasets, and checkpoints are intentionally excluded from this branch.

## Setup

Use Python 3.10 or newer. Install a PyTorch build that matches your CUDA setup, then install the project dependencies:

~~~bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
pip install -e .
~~~

If you do not install the package in editable mode, run commands with PYTHONPATH=src.

## Data Layout

The KITTI loader expects this layout by default:

~~~text
../Datasets/kitti_kaggle/
  train/
  test/
~~~

The bundled split files live in src/depth_estimation/data/kitti/dataloader/filenames/. To generate a new Eigen-style split file from a KITTI folder, use:

~~~bash
python scripts/create_kitti_split_file.py /path/to/kitti/test output/eigen_test_files.txt
~~~

NYU Depth V2 zip paths and normalization defaults are configured in configs/default.ini.

## Training

~~~bash
python -m depth_estimation.training.train --dataset-root ../Datasets/kitti_kaggle --epochs 15 --bs 4 --config configs/default.ini
~~~

Useful outputs:

- Checkpoints: models_kitti_1/checkpoint_epoch_<n>.pth.tar
- TensorBoard logs: runs/
- Training curve image: training_curves.png

To launch TensorBoard automatically during training, add --launch-tensorboard.

## Evaluation

~~~bash
python -m depth_estimation.evaluation.metrics --checkpoint models_kitti_1/checkpoint_epoch_14.pth.tar --dataset-root ../Datasets/kitti_kaggle
~~~

Save the best qualitative samples by REL score:

~~~bash
python -m depth_estimation.evaluation.best_cases --checkpoint models_kitti_1/checkpoint_epoch_14.pth.tar --dataset-root ../Datasets/kitti_kaggle --save-dir best_cases
~~~

## Notes

The repository is organized for reproducible code review and GitHub presentation. Large data files, experiment notebooks, event logs, and model weights should stay outside git and be referenced from local paths or release artifacts.
